    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d13">

                <pb facs="0273" n="15" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg15"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72119/119</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-02-19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 19, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received February 20, 5.10 p.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1649. Yours about Colonel House received.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d13-f1"/>
                    Favorable moment is passing. Germans have gained great victory over Russians,
                    are <pb facs="0274" n="16" xml:id="pg16"/> following it up and will soon turn
                    and break through French lines. Germany will never make proposal, but if Colonel
                    House can come here bringing secret reasonable proposal, it will be accepted in
                    all probability, but Germany will never pay an indemnity to Belgium and, on
                    contrary, will probably expect all or part of Congo and perhaps indemnity from
                    France for portion now occupied.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
